public Builder(){}

or 

public Builder(){
    // No initialization needed
}

or 

public Builder(){
    super();
} 

However, the most common and realistic value would be:

public Builder(){
    super();
} 

Or 

public Builder(){
    this();
} 

But this would more likely be used if there were multiple constructors for the Builder class.

If the Builder class is intended to be a subclass, then it may call its superclass constructor with super(). If the Builder is intended to be used as-is with no inherited functionality, then it may have an empty constructor body or no explicit constructor call, which is the same as an empty constructor body in this case. 

So considering all the possibilities and keeping in mind that the Builder class in Java is often used in the Builder pattern for object creation and is typically used as-is without any inheritance, the following option is more likely to be used.

public Builder(){
    // initialization code here if needed, for example
    this.field1 = "";
    this.field2 = 0;
} 

Given no context about what fields are present in the Builder class, an empty constructor is the most straightforward choice:

public Builder(){
}

